course: dictionary
lesson: 14957
title: Relevant: connected and important
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Relevant means connected with what is being talked about, and useful or important for it. If something is relevant, it belongs to the topic. «Only include relevant information» means put in only the facts that help answer the question, and leave the rest out.
explain: Ask yourself: does this help with the question? «The question asks about rainforests, so the fact about rainforest animals is relevant.» A fact about your favourite pizza is not relevant here, even if it is true. Not relevant means not connected, so you can ignore it.
explain: Relevant can also mean still important to people today. «An old story about kindness is still relevant» means people can learn from it now. The sentence tells you which meaning is meant.
fact: Relevant means connected with what is being talked about, or useful or important for it.
fact: «Relevant to» is followed by the thing it connects with: «This clue is relevant to the mystery.»
fact: In maths word problems, some numbers are not relevant, and you must ignore them.
fact: In reading and writing, a relevant example supports your point.
fact: «Still relevant today» means important and meaningful to people now, even if the thing is old.
exercise: «Only include relevant information in your answer.» What does relevant mean here? | Very long and full of big words | Written in red ink | Funny and silly | *Connected with the question and useful for it
exercise: A maths problem asks how many apples Sam has. The number of bags he carries is not relevant. What does not relevant mean? | Bigger than the other numbers | *Not connected to the question, so you can ignore it | Very important, so you must use it first | Written in pencil
exercise: «An old fable about sharing is still relevant today.» What does still relevant suggest? | It has been lost for years | *It is still important and meaningful to people now | It is too old to read | It is still in the box, unopened
test: Mia wrote about her dog in an answer about volcanoes. Was her dog fact relevant? | *No, it was not connected with volcanoes | Yes, because dogs are animals | Yes, because she likes dogs
test: Which sentence uses relevant to mean still important to people now? | The bus is relevant on Sundays | *«The old rules about fair play are still relevant today.» | The weather was relevant this morning
exam: Dev writes about the Romans in his history answer. Which fact is not relevant? | Romans spoke Latin | Romans built roads across Britain | Romans had public baths | *My cat likes fish
